@@ -18,8 +18,8 @@
 \begin{abstract}
 % BEGIN-EV2
 A buyer of computation often cannot see the quality of delivered work and can afford to check only a sample of it. We study a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, and free identity replacement, in a mechanism class without entry fees, identity certification, or collateral before receipts; a failed audit refunds the job's escrow and bans the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, immediate free replacement makes the ban costless against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, when re-entry comes before the next allocation. The rule we study, R3, checks every job of a new identity while the round's audits allow it, and after that window keeps targeting the newest identities, so a replacement can stay under heavy inspection: persistent age-ranked targeting rather than release by age. We report two registered tests. In the first, the original comparative claim failed; an amended claim, registered after development results were seen, passed on the public identity-replacement family. The second test compares R3 with a verified-history comparator C$^*$ at matched budget, payment, tolerance, ban, pacing, and sanctions, decomposes R3 into its three parts, adds stronger attacks, and repeats the B3--R3 comparison on six market instances drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
-Pooled over the six fresh markets, the registered procedure classifies R3 and C$^*$ as ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_q1_est} under D2+H3 and \site{abs_q1_est_x} under X7-SC, almost all of it in the profit the attacker earns when it deviates. The registered intervals hold the six markets fixed; treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] under D2+H3 (exploratory), so neither the ordering nor the equivalence extends to the neighbourhood. The X7-SC search returned the D2+H3 winner it starts from in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} Q1 cells, so the two families are close to one test. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, not on attacker gain. R3's two targeting parts are substitutes (factorial contrast \site{abs_q2_pg}): once newest-first targeting is in place, the priority phase changes the gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}; pacing slightly raises the measured gain. At the headline market, the selected sealed-attacker policy gains \site{abs_q3_f111_h4} against R3; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{abs_q3_w0}, and similarly for B3 and C$^*$; and an exploratory lottery between a welfare attack and a profitable attack, selected on the evaluation records, has estimated normalised gain \site{abs_ph_mix_gain} and gross efficiency \site{abs_ph_mix_eff} against R3, with its population profitability not established. The B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of the six fresh markets, all of them qualified as results of the prescribed search procedure.
-All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references and are conditional on the sampled markets, the stated neighbourhood, the fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and a population whose honest providers never replace their identities.
+Pooled over the six fresh markets, the registered procedure classifies R3 and C$^*$ as ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{abs_q1_est} under D2+H3 and \site{abs_q1_est_x} under X7-SC, almost all of it in the profit the attacker earns when it deviates. The registered intervals hold the six markets fixed; treated as a sample of markets, the per-market differences give a $t$ interval of [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] under D2+H3 (exploratory), so this analysis does not establish the ordering or the equivalence for the neighbourhood. The X7-SC search returned the D2+H3 winner it starts from in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} Q1 cells, so the two families are close to one test. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the runner-up left a lower development gain. R3's two targeting parts are substitutes (factorial contrast \site{abs_q2_pg}): once newest-first targeting is in place, the priority phase changes the gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}; pacing slightly raises the measured gain. At the headline market, the selected sealed-attacker policy gains \site{abs_q3_f111_h4} against R3, the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{abs_q3_w0}, and similarly for B3 and C$^*$, and the registered welfare search under a profitability constraint found no profitable attack against R3. The B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of the six fresh markets, all of them qualified as results of the prescribed search procedure.
+All claims concern the prescribed searches and frozen references and are conditional on the sampled markets, the stated neighbourhood, the fixed rule parameters, the searched attacker families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities.
 % END-EV2
 \end{abstract}
 
@@ -35,7 +35,7 @@ We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identi
 
 \paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.
 
-\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification holds for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) At the headline market, the registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against R3 (its selected policy has zero estimated gain), and an exploratory lottery between the unconstrained welfare attack and a profitable attack reaches substantially lower empirical gross efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; this follows from averaging the two components, and its population profitability remains unestablished. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
+\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) At the headline market, the registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against R3 (its selected policy has zero estimated gain), and an exploratory lottery between the unconstrained welfare attack and a profitable attack reaches substantially lower empirical gross efficiency at non-negative estimated gain; this follows from averaging the two components, and its population profitability remains unestablished. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
 % BEGIN-EV2
 \paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3: R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and the difference varies in sign across markets. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
 % END-EV2
@@ -60,7 +60,7 @@ We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identi
 
 \paragraph{Audit budget.} Let $\hat S$ be the expected honest first-best surplus over $T$ rounds and $m = \hat S / n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ the mean surplus per first-best served job. The operator's audit budget is $B = \beta \hat S$ and the audit cost $\alpha = \rho m$, so the allowance is $B/\alpha = (\beta/\rho)\, n_{\mathrm{FB}}$ audits; unspent budget carries over.
 
-\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Honest providers never replace their identities, so in this model a new identity is always a deviating one, except in the honest-replacement diagnostic of the second test; honest churn is outside the model (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).
+\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Every identity is new at the start. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity belongs to a provider that has replaced, usually the focal one; a truthful focal provider may also replace after a false failure, so a new identity marks a replacement history, not dishonest delivery. Churn of honest identities is outside the model, except for the single replacement of the second test's honest-replacement diagnostic (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).
 
 \paragraph{Admissible rules.} A rule reads only the current offers and job values, the audit results it bought, its own memory, and the configuration. Payments are funded by buyer escrow. The only consequences are a refund of up to the escrow of an audited job that failed; a ban of the identity for at most 50 rounds; holding back up to three rounds of an identity's receipts as a bond; and allocation priority and audit selection as functions of the allowed inputs. There are no entry fees, no collateral before receipts, no identity certification, and no consequences for unaudited jobs.
 
