257:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). R3's selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C$^*$ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records, and they show that the W1 policy class is not closed under randomisation. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the W1 policy class is not closed under randomisation; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
260:\caption{Exploratory, after the report: at the headline market, the efficiency of the selected W1 and W0 attacks, and, for each companion family, the episode-level lottery between the W0 attack and a profitable attack of the same rule (two displayed companions, H4 and D2+H3; all five examined companions are in \texttt{posthoc.json}) at the largest W0 weight on a grid of step \const{0.0005} whose mean gain keeps a non-negative nominal one-sided \const{95} percent lower bound, unadjusted for selection; estimated gain, that bound, and the mixture's efficiency, all on the evaluation records that selected the weight; ``Train gain'' is the mixture's mean gain on the training seeds (the registered W1 constraint requires it to be non-negative), reconstructed from the search archives. The row marked training-feasible, added after external review, uses the largest grid weight at which both the evaluation bound and the training gain are non-negative. B3's W0 attack has a positive estimated gain.}\label{tab:ph-welfare}
289:\paragraph{Secondary comparison.} At the headline market, with each rule's own tuned parameters, the gains are \site{r_sec_b3t} (B3-t), \site{r_sec_r3t} (R3-t), and \site{r_sec_cst} (C$^*$-t) under D2+H3. This comparison is secondary and descriptive.
325:\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities, which would need a matched comparison with replacement disabled. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
335:For the uniform-audit baselines with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; across markets the difference varies in sign, and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search yields gross efficiency near \site{c_w1} for both rules. Exploratory episode-level mixtures selected on the evaluation records yield, with H4, estimated efficiencies of \site{c_mx_r3} for R3 and \site{c_mx_cs} for C$^*$; public D2+H3 mixtures meeting the empirical training constraint yield \site{c_mx_r3_p} and \site{c_mx_cs_p}, respectively; all at non-negative estimated evaluation gain. The registered W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies; the population profitability of the selected mixtures remains unestablished. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
348:\paragraph{Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}.} Let $V_{\mathrm{clean}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{fail}}$ be the best continuation values after no focal failure and after at least one, so $C_U = V_{\mathrm{clean}} - V_{\mathrm{fail}}$. By the first condition, immediate free replacement attains the clean distribution of future states after a failure, so $V_{\mathrm{fail}} \ge V_{\mathrm{clean}}$. By the second, any plan after a failure (keeping the banned identity and waiting, or replacing at any later time) can be copied after no failure, so $V_{\mathrm{fail}} \le V_{\mathrm{clean}}$. Hence $C_U = 0$. For B2 and B3 the second condition holds because a failed identity carries only its ban, with no priority or payment privilege, a clean provider can copy waiting by not offering and can itself replace its identity, no bond is lost, and the audit budget and rival states do not depend on the focal identity's history; replacement restores eligibility before the next allocation. Refunds of the failed jobs in the current round still enter Lemma~\ref{lem:local}. A rule that gave priority to an identity that waits out its ban would violate the second condition, and the corollary would not apply to it.
